WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.480
This episode of Real Engineering is brought
to you by Brilliant.org a problem solving

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.000
website that helps you think like an engineer.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:12.380
The sight of a 9.4 tonne plane slowly rise
off the ground, balancing precariously on

00:00:12.380 --> 00:00:17.660
4 columns of air over the rough water of Galway
bay, is the closest to sci-fi alien technology

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:19.390
I have ever seen in my life.

00:00:19.390 --> 00:00:23.279
An inspiring sight that seeded an obsession
with aviation technology.

00:00:23.279 --> 00:00:27.250
The harrier was the culmination of decades
of bizarre experimentation to eliminate the

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:31.779
planes greatest weakness, it’s need for
a runway to both land and takeoff.

00:00:31.779 --> 00:00:35.760
This fascinating technology has one of the
richest histories in aviation with roots in

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:40.280
both the lunar lander and outlandish WW2 era
German sketches.

00:00:40.280 --> 00:00:45.050
Both the Allies and the Germans used helicopters
and autogyros during this period, but using

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:48.709
a rotor for both lift and thrust inherently
trades off speed.

00:00:48.709 --> 00:00:52.549
The German’s dreamed of a plane capable
of taking off from anywhere, eliminating the

00:00:52.549 --> 00:00:58.350
need for tactically vulnerable airstrips,
without sacrificing speed or dogfight capabilities.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.969
Inspired by their ballistic missile program,
which still lacked effective guidance programs

00:01:01.969 --> 00:01:06.710
to accurate target allied aircraft, Erich
Bachem developed the tail-sitting rocket powered

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.509
BP-349, which would allow the pilot to take
control of the plane in it’s final stages

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:15.280
of flight to guide it to it’s target and
unleash it’s barrage of smaller rockets

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:17.140
before ejecting to safety.

00:01:17.140 --> 00:01:21.570
The only manned test of this aircraft resulted
in the death of the test pilot and the idea

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:26.170
was abandoned, but that didn’t stopped the
Germans in their pursuit of VTOL aircraft.

00:01:26.170 --> 00:01:30.810
Next up was an even more bizarre tail sitting
aircraft that featured 3 giant propellor blades

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:35.240
each powered by ram-jets at their tips, this
would function similar to a helicopter for

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:39.759
take off and then transition to forward flight
and use the blades like a giant propellor,

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:43.950
but it would require a nose-up position to
achieve adequate lift, as it didn’t have

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:44.950
any wings.

00:01:44.950 --> 00:01:48.670
Unsurprisingly this didn’t make it passed
basic wind tunnel testing, but could well

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:54.850
have inspired the Lockheed XFV and Convair
XFV Pogo planes in the 1950s which used massive

00:01:54.850 --> 00:01:57.210
counter-rotating turbo-propellers.

00:01:57.210 --> 00:02:00.869
The Lockheed version only ever managed to
hover for a brief moment while transitioning

00:02:00.869 --> 00:02:06.030
from horizontal flight to an upwards vertical
flight, but the Convair XFV Pogo flown by

00:02:06.030 --> 00:02:10.340
Lieutenant Colonel James Coleman became the
first aircraft in history to successful fly

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.349
in both forward aerodynamic flight and in
hover.

00:02:13.349 --> 00:02:17.450
Ultimately both planes were cancelled due
the difficulty in flying them, and their lack

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:20.030
of speed and lack of lifting power.

00:02:20.030 --> 00:02:23.440
Engines for propellor driven aircraft were
simply not powerful enough yet.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.599
To achieve necessary lift the rotors would
need to increase velocity or diameter, which

00:02:27.599 --> 00:02:31.800
would decrease the max horizontal speed of
the aircraft as the tip-speeds of the blades

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:35.430
would rise, meaning the tips of the blades
could easily break the sound barrier and cause

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:37.270
all kinds of problems.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.530
This problem and how the incredibly versatile
V-22 Osprey solved it needs a video by itself

00:02:41.530 --> 00:02:45.819
to explain the nuanced design of helicopters
and their speed limits, but for now you will

00:02:45.819 --> 00:02:48.510
just need to trust me that it’s a difficult
problem to solve.

00:02:48.510 --> 00:02:52.080
And with the advent of powerful jet-engines,
the problem was largely solved without the

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:53.840
need for large propellers.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:58.799
The first craft to use jet propulsion for
vertical lift was the Rolls-Royce Thrust Measuring

00:02:58.799 --> 00:03:02.920
Rig, aptly nicknamed the flying bedstead,
for it’s obvious departure from the bird

00:03:02.920 --> 00:03:04.550
like designs of the past.

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:08.360
This configuration largely influenced the
design of the Lunar Lander training vehicle

00:03:08.360 --> 00:03:12.310
that NASA developed for Neil Armstrong and
other astronauts to practice with.

00:03:12.310 --> 00:03:16.670
They mounted the engine on a gimbal to ensure
it’s thrust always pointed directly downwards

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:20.620
and only provided enough lift to simulate
the moon’s gravity, while hydrogen peroxide

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:22.430
rockets were used for control.

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:26.549
Neil Armstrong attributed the success of his
difficult landing on the moon to this ingenious

00:03:26.549 --> 00:03:27.870
training vehicle.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:31.830
The lessons learned through early research
craft like these provided Rolls-Royce with

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:36.620
the knowledge required to develop the Rolls-Royce
Pegasus engine, which powers the Harrier.

00:03:36.620 --> 00:03:41.060
This engine needed to have enough thrust to
lift the entire weight of the plane, the designers

00:03:41.060 --> 00:03:45.450
of the plane made this job easier by utilizing
carbon-fibre composites for much aircrafts

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:50.069
structures to save weight, making the Harrier
one of the first planes to use these materials.

00:03:50.069 --> 00:03:54.930
Yet the job of designing a single engine capable
of providing enough vertical thrust was still

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:58.260
difficult, and the resulting engine is pretty
unique as a result.

00:03:58.260 --> 00:04:01.900
The engine is similar to a traditional jet
engine in that it consists of a low pressure

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.650
compressor fan, a high pressure compressor,
a combustion chamber, a high pressure turbine

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:08.170
and a low pressure turbine.

00:04:08.170 --> 00:04:12.829
Where it differs radically is the engine outlet
is not one large opening, but split into four

00:04:12.829 --> 00:04:16.510
where the first 2 nozzles duct some of the
air coming from the low pressure compressor

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:19.500
and the final two duct air from the higher
pressure turbines.

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.820
[2]
Because the air bleeding from the low pressure

00:04:21.820 --> 00:04:25.890
compressor system has less force than the
high pressure nozzles, the low pressure nozzles

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:30.350
need to be placed further from the planes
centre of gravity than the high pressure nozzles.

00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:34.140
This balances the plane along the length of
the plane, but in vertical take-off mode there

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:38.170
is no air-flowing over the wings to provide
force for the control surfaces.

00:04:38.170 --> 00:04:42.670
So the plane needs a way of controlling it’s
roll, pitch and yaw in vertical takeoff mode,

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.630
and so the plane features nozzles that bleed
air from the engine on the nose, tail and

00:04:46.630 --> 00:04:47.750
wing tips.

00:04:47.750 --> 00:04:52.330
This control system is not as reliable as
aerodynamic lift, which is largely self correcting

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.080
when disturbances occurs, although fighters
tend to purposely decrease stability to increase

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:01.110
maneuverability, which requires a computer
to constantly monitor, but the disturbances

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:05.140
due to ground effect from the air from it’s
own jet can cause oscillations that overwhelm

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:09.010
this control system, and with no accurate
way of correcting them, they could grow until

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:13.250
the plane inverts and lands on the cockpit,
which has killed pilots on several occasions.

00:05:13.250 --> 00:05:16.910
So pilots often dropped the plane heavily
from a few metres above the ground before

00:05:16.910 --> 00:05:21.280
the oscillations could take hold, which obviously
wasn’t ideal for the landing gear [3]

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:26.130
The harrier was also disadvantaged to conventional
aircraft as the vertical takeoff mode it was

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:30.780
severely limited in max take-off weight and
burnt off much of it’s fuel in this intensive

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.250
maneuver, reducing it’s range too.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:37.631
So most Harrier take offs take place only
as partial vertical take-off,where the plane

00:05:37.631 --> 00:05:41.620
would accelerate on the runway like a conventional
plane to achieve some lift from the wings

00:05:41.620 --> 00:05:45.600
and then angle the nozzles to achieve the
final lift needed to safely take off on the

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.050
shorter runways of aircraft carriers.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:51.940
The plane could then land vertically without
wasting as much fuel later on when the weight

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:55.600
of the aircraft had reduced through use of
it’s fuel and armaments.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.780
This takes it’s max take-off weight from
9,415 kg to 14,100 kg, which is still lackluster

00:06:00.780 --> 00:06:05.950
when comparing to their fellow Marine aircraft
like the F/A-18 hornet that has a max take-off

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:12.530
weight of 23,541 kg and a maximum speed of
1.8 compared to the harriers subsonic, 0.9.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:17.030
Make it a much more capable attack platform,
but the hornet is only capable of launching

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:21.220
from large carriers, whereas the Harrier can
launch from amphibious assault ships like

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:22.760
the USS America.

00:06:22.760 --> 00:06:26.750
The Harrier has even managed to land on a
cargo ship when it’s pilot lost radio contact

00:06:26.750 --> 00:06:28.610
with his crew and ran out of fuel.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:33.260
But both of these fighters are slated to be
replaced by the controversial F-35, which

00:06:33.260 --> 00:06:38.370
will be capable of VTOL, thanks to incredible
directional rear thrust and shaft driven lift

00:06:38.370 --> 00:06:39.370
fan.

00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:43.240
This fighter improves on much of the technology
that the Harrier laid the ground for while

00:06:43.240 --> 00:06:48.220
including futuristic VR helmets allowing the
pilot to see through their own plane using

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:53.280
the 6 infrared cameras surrounding the plane,
advanced laser targeting and radar capabilities,

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.890
all the while incorporating stealth technology.

00:06:55.890 --> 00:07:00.220
The plane has been deeply entrenched in US
politics, raking up huge development costs

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:05.010
and supporting thousands of American jobs,
it has been delayed again and again, largely

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:08.730
due to its ambitious technological leap over
it older counterparts.

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:13.310
Should it finally reach service it will become
the most advanced plane in the history of

00:07:13.310 --> 00:07:14.310
mankind.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:17.820
Each advancement like this is built upon the
foundations of physics that humankind has

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:20.220
learned over the course of our existence.

00:07:20.220 --> 00:07:23.581
If you would like to flex your physics knowledge
and learn more about the principles of the

00:07:23.581 --> 00:07:25.980
universe upon which these machines are built.

